libgcc/libgomp IRIX runtime adjustments

libgcov maps anonymous memory from /dev/zero because IRIX has no
MAP_ANONYMOUS. libgomp reads _SC_NPROC_ONLN instead of _SC_NPROCESSORS_ONLN
and links itself against -lpthread.

The pdaxrom source diff also carried gthr-posix.h hunks commenting out
IRIX's pthread_rwlock_*, pthread_equal, sched_yield and
pthread_mutexattr_settype references "because IRIX lacks them"; dropping
the settype call left libgcc's recursive mutexes as plain mutexes (audit
finding C14). The captured 6.5.7m headers contradict that premise:
usr/include/pthread.h declares pthread_equal (with the identity macro),
pthread_mutexattr_settype and PTHREAD_MUTEX_RECURSIVE, sys/pthread.h
defines pthread_rwlock_t while pthread.h declares the pthread_rwlock_*
operations, and usr/include/sched.h declares sched_yield, which the
captured o32 and n32 libc.so export as a weak symbol. Every gthr-posix.h
hunk is therefore dropped and the file stays at the upstream release; the
target genuinely lacks only the static recursive initialisers, which
upstream already selects __gthread_recursive_mutex_init_function for.
The pthread entry points live in libpthread.so, now listed in
scripts/rig/sysroot.files; scripts/test-gthread-recursive.sh links
against it on the guest and fails when a symbol is missing, and
scripts/rig/test-gthread-patch.py checks the selection against the
capture, so no capability is silently commented out.

Provenance: pdaxrom/irix-gcc tag 1.2 (2aa3421b), gcc-15.2.0-irix.diff.

diff --git a/libgcc/libgcov.h b/libgcc/libgcov.h
index 1643b4440..eaa1bb499 100644
--- a/libgcc/libgcov.h
+++ b/libgcc/libgcov.h
@@ -46,7 +46,10 @@
 #include "gcov.h"
 
 #if HAVE_SYS_MMAN_H
+#include <sys/types.h>
 #include <sys/mman.h>
+#include <sys/stat.h>
+#include <fcntl.h>
 #endif
 
 #if __CHAR_BIT__ == 8
@@ -427,8 +430,14 @@ gcov_counter_add (gcov_type *counter, gcov_type value,
 static inline void *
 malloc_mmap (size_t length)
 {
-  return mmap (NULL, length, PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE,
-	       MAP_PRIVATE | MAP_ANONYMOUS, -1, 0);
+  int fd = open("/dev/zero", O_RDWR);
+  if (fd < 0)
+    return MAP_FAILED;
+  void *ret = mmap (NULL, length, PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE,
+	       MAP_PRIVATE, fd, 0);
+
+  close(fd);
+  return ret;
 }
 
 #endif
diff --git a/libgomp/config/posix/proc.c b/libgomp/config/posix/proc.c
index 95955cc41..61c130131 100644
--- a/libgomp/config/posix/proc.c
+++ b/libgomp/config/posix/proc.c
@@ -38,6 +38,9 @@
 # endif
 #endif
 
+#ifdef __sgi
+#define _SC_NPROCESSORS_ONLN _SC_NPROC_ONLN
+#endif
 
 /* At startup, determine the default number of threads.  It would seem
    this should be related to the number of cpus online.  */
diff --git a/libgomp/configure.tgt b/libgomp/configure.tgt
index 46af75f97..a1e4a107d 100644
--- a/libgomp/configure.tgt
+++ b/libgomp/configure.tgt
@@ -153,6 +153,11 @@ case "${target}" in
 	config_path="bsd darwin posix"
 	;;
 
+  *-*-irix6*)
+	# Need to link with -lpthread so libgomp.so is self-contained.
+	XLDFLAGS="${XLDFLAGS} -lpthread"
+	;;
+
   *-*-freebsd*)
 	# Need to link with -lpthread so libgomp.so is self-contained.
 	XLDFLAGS="${XLDFLAGS} -lpthread"
